The most modern and clean approach to vertically center variable-height content inside a div is to use flexbox on the parent container. Here’s how:

**For a fixed-height container:**
```html
<div class="container">
  <div class="content">
    <!-- Your variable-height content here -->
  </div>
</div>
```
```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center; /* Centers content vertically */
  height: fixed_value; /* Set your fixed height here */
}
.content {
  /* No need for specific height; it will expand as needed */
}
```

**For a variable-height container:**
```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  /* height: omit or set to auto; flex will handle proportions */
}
```
This method works for both cases and doesn’t require hacks or extra markup. It’s also fully semantic and future-proof.

P.S.: This approach uses only standard CSS features and is widely supported in all modern browsers.

